<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- TemporalWindow — ONE mode's settings panel. Hosted in its own OS window by WindowManager (the native
     caption is the only chrome), so there is no panel frame: the root is the dark surface, stretched to fill
     the window.

       ┌─────────────────────────────────────────[lock][pin]─┐
       │  ┌──┐ ForeCast                    │ MDT 4/5          │  ← NO window title: each CONTENT opens with
       │  └──┘ Day 1 · Wed Sep 2           │ next ~9 min      │    a TemporalWindows/TemporalWindowHeader
       │  (fixed 480 × full height; the CONTENT scrolls itself)│
       │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
       │  │                                              │    │
       │  │   that mode's CONTENT — ONE UserControl from │    │
       │  │   Controls/Composites, all three declared    │    │
       │  │   below, only Mode's one x:Load'ed           │    │
       │  │   TemporalWindowNowCastContent               │    │
       │  │   TemporalWindowPastCastContent              │    │
       │  │   TemporalWindowForeCastContent              │    │
       │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
       └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

     ⚠️ ONE CLASS, THREE WINDOWS. This is registered three times (MainWindow), once per TemporalMode, each
     with its own id, title, size and open flag. The alternative — three near-identical window classes — is
     three copies of one layout waiting to drift apart, which is the thing this repo keeps deleting.
     NAMES READ AS THE TREE (2026-10-16): TemporalWindow → TemporalWindow<Mode>Content → TemporalWindowHeader
     → TemporalWindowHeader<Mode>Readout; each x:Name equals its type name.

     WHAT THIS REPLACED, and why: ONE window with the three modes as TABS, opened by a shared three-dot key.
     That existed because the three mode keys each owned a window and so meant two things at once — "turn the
     mode on" when unlit, "flip my window" when lit — with the window's open state visible nowhere. The keys
     are SPLIT now (Controls/Primitives/SplitTemporalToggle): the square runs the mode, the rail at its foot
     latches this window, and both states are on screen. That fixes the overloading without collapsing the
     windows, which is what buys back the tabs' one real cost — NowCast and ForeCast coexist as modes, so
     their panels can be parked on two monitors again.
     ⚠️ Do NOT re-absorb these into a tabbed panel. See MapViewModel's temporal region before trying.

     ⚠️ THIS WINDOW CANNOT OUTLIVE ITS MODE. Its rail can only be clicked while the mode runs, and
     MapViewModel.OnTemporalModesChanged closes the window when the mode stops — so there is no "every tab
     greyed" state to design for and no empty state to show. The panel is only ever up when it has something
     to act on, which is why nothing in these Contents needs to check whether its mode is running.
     ⚠️ A CONTENT NEVER STARTS ITS MODE — that is the key's job, and it always was. The date/start/window fields
     drive a replay that is already armed, the watch/warning toggles overlay a live loop, the day/product
     selectors pick which outlook is drawn.

     ⚠️ ALL THREE CONTENTS ARE DECLARED BELOW, each behind x:Load; only Mode's one is ever built (the others
     stay unrealized — no cost). Mode and ViewModel are CONSTRUCTOR arguments, set before InitializeComponent,
     so the OneTime x:Load binds see the final Mode on their one evaluation. x:Load's real hazard — bound
     OneWay to a bool it UNLOADS again when that bool goes false, throwing away live widget state — cannot
     happen: the binds are OneTime and Mode is read-only. Don't make Mode settable or the binds OneWay.

     ⚠️ THE PIN AND LOCK ARE WIRED IN CODE-BEHIND: which flag each drives depends on Mode, which an x:Bind
     path cannot express (no method call with an argument on a two-way bind). See the constructor. -->
<UserControl x:Class="Anvil.Controls.TemporalWindows.TemporalWindow"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:tw="using:Anvil.Controls.TemporalWindows"
             xmlns:ctl="using:Anvil.Controls.Primitives"
             xmlns:comp="using:Anvil.Controls.Composites"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             mc:Ignorable="d">

    <!-- Dark surface fills the whole window. -->
    <!-- ⚠️ NO TITLE ROW any more: every Content opens with its own TemporalWindowHeader (status square · name over a date line ·
         a readout), so the mode's name shows once, in the Content (ForeCast was the last, 2026-10-07). -->
    <Grid Background="{ThemeResource OverlayBarSurfaceBrush}">

        <!-- ⚠️ NO ScrollViewer HERE. The window opens at the FULL height above the bottom chrome (WindowManager's
             edge anchors), so a Content shorter than that just shows surface below it. A Content scrolls ITSELF with
             Primitives/PanelScroller (all three Contents have it), because only the Content knows what stays fixed —
             PastCast's Timeframe and NowCast's clock + tiles don't scroll, their sections do; ForeCast's
             discussion stays pinned below its scrolling outlook.
             Each Content stretches to the full row height: Past/Now top-align their own roots and are
             unaffected, ForeCast stretches so its legend can fill what is left. -->
        <tw:TemporalWindowPastCastContent x:Name="TemporalWindowPastCastContent" x:Load="{x:Bind IsPast}" Margin="16,14,16,14"
                          ViewModel="{x:Bind ViewModel}"/>
        <tw:TemporalWindowNowCastContent x:Name="TemporalWindowNowCastContent" x:Load="{x:Bind IsNow}" Margin="16,14,16,14"
                         ViewModel="{x:Bind ViewModel}"/>
        <tw:TemporalWindowForeCastContent x:Name="TemporalWindowForeCastContent" x:Load="{x:Bind IsFore}" Margin="16,14,16,14"
                          ViewModel="{x:Bind ViewModel}"/>

        <!-- Lock + pin. Bound in code-behind — the flags they drive depend on Mode. -->
        <ctl:LockToggle x:Name="Lock"/>
        <ctl:PinToggle x:Name="Pin"/>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
